   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.2
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "Meet Thurgood Marshall.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Thurgood Marshall",
    "sub": "1908-1993"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "He was born in Baltimore in 1908. After college, he wanted to study law at home.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "young",
    "caption": "Thurgood Marshall, 1925"
   },
   "section": "story",
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Baltimore",
     "sub": "Maryland"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "refused",
   "text": "But the law school in his own state, the University of Maryland, did not admit Black students.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Refused",
    "sub": "University of Maryland law school"
   },
   "section": "twist",
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1930"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "howard",
   "text": "So he went to Howard University in Washington instead, and graduated first in his class in 1933.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "First in his class",
    "sub": "Howard Law, 1933"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "houston",
   "text": "His teacher there, Charles Hamilton Houston, trained lawyers to fight segregation in court.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "houston",
    "caption": "Charles Hamilton Houston"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "murray",
   "text": "Two years later, Marshall and Houston sued the school that had shut him out, for a young Black man named Donald Murray.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "thirties",
    "caption": "Thurgood Marshall, 1930s"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "won",
   "text": "They won. Murray was admitted to the University of Maryland law school.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Admitted",
    "sub": "Donald Murray, 1936"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "brown",
   "text": "Then Marshall took on segregated schools across the country. In 1954, in Brown versus Board of Education, the Supreme Court ruled for him, nine to nothing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "brown",
    "caption": "Hayes, Marshall and Nabrit after the Brown decision, 1954"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "court",
   "text": "In 1967, he became the first Black justice on the Supreme Court. He served for twenty-four years.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "court",
    "caption": "The Supreme Court, 1967"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "library",
   "text": "In 2019, Maryland renamed its state law library after him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "painting",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.25
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "end",
   "text": "They kept him out. He opened the door for everyone.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "portrait",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.25
    ],
    "zoom": [
